<p>An Iberian ham label contains several pieces of information that are often mixed together in everyday conversation: breed percentage, feeding and management category, legal sales description and, on whole hams and shoulders, the colour seal. They are connected, but they do not mean the same thing. Understanding that distinction makes it much easier to buy intelligently.</p>
<p>When a product says 50%, 75% or 100% Iberian, it is describing <strong>breed composition</strong>: the proportion of Iberian genetic ancestry in the animal. That number alone does not tell you whether the pig was acorn-fed, free-range cebo de campo, or linked to a protected designation of origin. Breed percentage should never be read in isolation.</p>
<h2>What the rules require before a product can be called Iberian</h2>
<p>Spain's Iberian quality standard, Royal Decree 4/2014, sets requirements for traceability, breed and, where relevant, feeding and management. To use the racial designation Iberian, the animal must have at least 50% Iberian breed ancestry.</p>
<p>The description 100% Iberian is reserved for animals whose genealogy is entirely Iberian under the control system established by the standard. Products that are not 100% Iberian must state the Iberian breed percentage visibly. The aim is to make the genetic information clear rather than allowing marketing language to replace it.</p>
<h2>What 50% Iberian means</h2>
<p>A 50% Iberian product comes from an animal with half Iberian ancestry. The authorised breeding system uses Iberian maternal lines and permitted Duroc crosses for certain categories. The finished product can still be acorn-fed Iberian, cebo de campo Iberian or cebo Iberian if it satisfies the relevant feeding and management conditions.</p>
<p>This is the key distinction: <strong>50% Iberian does not mean 50% acorn-fed</strong>. Breed and feeding are separate variables. A 50% Iberian ham can qualify as bellota if the pig meets the legal requirements for that category.</p>
<h2>What 75% Iberian means</h2>
<p>Seventy-five per cent Iberian describes an intermediate level of Iberian ancestry between 50% and 100%. Consumers do not need to memorise every possible pedigree combination. The important point is that the percentage must be backed by the applicable genealogy and traceability controls.</p>
<p>As with 50%, a 75% Iberian product can appear under different feeding categories. Two hams can therefore share the same breed percentage while having different sales descriptions because one is bellota and another cebo de campo.</p>
<h2>What 100% Iberian means</h2>
<p>The 100% Iberian designation means the animal is pure Iberian according to the standard. If it also meets the bellota requirements, the complete description is bellota 100% Iberian and whole hams and shoulders use the black seal. Spanish rules also reserve the expression pata negra for this specific bellota 100% Iberian category.</p>
<p>That does not mean any package with a large 100% claim is automatically bellota. Read the full legal description. Correct information combines product type, feeding and management category, and breed designation.</p>
<h2>Breed and feeding answer two different questions</h2>
<p>A simple way to read the label is to ask two questions. First: what proportion of the animal is Iberian breed? That gives 50%, 75% or 100%. Second: how was the animal fed and managed in the phase that defines the commercial category? That gives bellota, cebo de campo or cebo.</p>
<p>On whole hams, the seal colour summarises part of that combination. Black means bellota 100% Iberian; red means bellota Iberian that is not 100%; green means cebo de campo Iberian; and white means cebo Iberian. Looking only at colour or only at breed percentage gives an incomplete picture.</p>
<h2>Is 100% Iberian always better than 50%?</h2>
<p>The word better needs context. Breed percentage describes genetics, not a sensory score. Feeding, animal age, raw material, salting, drying, maturation, curing time, storage and personal preference all influence the finished ham.</p>
<p>Bellota 100% Iberian is a very specific regulatory category and represents the maximum Iberian breed percentage. But turning that percentage into an automatic flavour ranking oversimplifies the product. Some consumers prefer very intense, fatty profiles while others value a different balance of texture and flavour.</p>
<h2>What to read before buying</h2>
<p>Instead of searching for one attractive word, find the complete sales description. It should tell you whether the product is ham or shoulder, whether it is bellota, cebo de campo or cebo, and what the breed designation is. If it is not 100% Iberian, the percentage should appear close to that description.</p>
<p>Then consider producer, format, approximate weight where relevant, whole versus sliced presentation and any protected designation of origin. Each piece of information answers a different buying question.</p>
<h2>Slicing does not change the breed percentage</h2>
<p>A bellota 100% Iberian ham remains in that category when sold boneless or sliced, provided traceability is maintained. Presentation changes storage and serving convenience, not genetic origin. The same principle applies to 50% and 75% Iberian products.</p>
<p>This matters when comparing a whole leg with packets. Sliced ham may be more practical for occasional consumption, while a whole leg suits frequent serving and the carving experience. The legal category, however, should be interpreted in the same way.</p>
<h2>How to use the percentage when deciding</h2>
<p>Breed percentage is valuable when read with the rest of the label. If you specifically want the strictest combination of pure Iberian genetics and acorn feeding, look for bellota 100% Iberian. If you are comparing bellota products with different breed percentages, also consider producer, format, curing, protected origin and budget.</p>
<p>Buying well is not about chasing the highest number without context. It is about understanding what each piece of information means and choosing the product that fits how you plan to enjoy it.</p>
